    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d343">

                <pb facs="0283" n="274" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg274"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.24 Raw Materials/1132: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-06-20T18:00:00+01:00">June 20, 1940—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 20—12:48 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1753. Upon receipt of your No. 1277, June 19, noon, the situation was discussed
                    with the Colonial Office which made available Hay’s telegram and the proposed
                    reply which was drafted by the Colonial Office after consultation with Miller
                    and Milne and which no doubt will be approved by an interdepartmental committee
                    meeting today. As originally drafted this reply for the most part emphasized the
                    impracticability of acting on Hay’s suggestion without offering a constructive
                    alternative suggestion. But after some discussion the draft was altered to
                    include an offer to take the necessary steps provided the United States
                    guaranteed a minimum price pending the conclusion of the final arrangement; this
                    was designed to overcome the difficulty of rubber exporters being unable or
                    unwilling to ship without some immediate monetary consideration.</p>
                <pb facs="0284" n="275" xml:id="pg275"/>
                <p>The Colonial Office seems well disposed and the Embassy took occasion to
                    emphasize that not only is quick action needed but that for the first time
                    powers are available which permit action and that the traditional inabilities of
                    the past should be put aside.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
